From: Tara Nath Subedi <Tara.Subedi@windriver.com>
Date: Fri, 07 Aug 2026 16:46:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] GM <–L3–> BC1 <–L2–> BC2; BC1 ClockClass remains 6 after GM loss

Update parentDS when port assumes grand master role

In handle_state_decision_event(), clock_update_grandmaster() is only
called when best == NULL (no foreign master on any port). The
PS_GRAND_MASTER case in the per-port BMCA loop does not update
c->dad.pds. This creates a window where c->dad.pds retains stale data:

When a BC node loses its upstream GM, the peer BC may briefly send an
announce with its own local clockClass (e.g. 248). The receiving BC
sees this inferior clockClass, BMCA determines local is better, and
returns PS_GRAND_MASTER. However, best != NULL (the peer's announce is
still a valid foreign master record), so clock_update_grandmaster() at
the top of the function is skipped. No port gets PS_SLAVE either, so
clock_update_slave() is not called. Neither function updates c->dad.pds
which still holds the old GM data (clockClass 6) from the previous
BMCA cycle when the port was SLAVE.

The first announce sent after the transition (~1ms) reads from this
stale c->dad.pds and propagates clockClass 6 to the peer BC, which
re-slaves to it. Once stable in MASTER state on both ports, no further
SDE is triggered and c->dad.pds is never corrected.

The fix calls clock_update_grandmaster() in the PS_GRAND_MASTER case,
ensuring parentDS reflects local GM settings before the first announce
is sent. This is safe because:
- PS_GRAND_MASTER and PS_SLAVE are mutually exclusive in a single BMCA
  cycle (if local is better than clock_best, no port gets PS_SLAVE)
- clock_update_grandmaster() is idempotent (clock_compare_pds guards
  against spurious notifications)
- inhibit_announce ports never receive PS_GRAND_MASTER from BMCA

Test Plan:
- Setup: GM <--L3--> BC1 <--L2--> BC2 (T-BC topology)
- Verify steady state: both BCs report gm.ClockClass=6, lock-state=LOCKED
- Stop GM traffic (disable unicast grants or disconnect GM)
- Verify within 5s:
  - BC1/BC2 clockclass no more stuck to 6, degrades (e.g. 248, 135, 165)
  - all port state: MASTER, PARENT_DATA_SET follows its GRANDMASTER_SETTINGS_NP
  - one port state: SLAVE, PARENT_DATA_SET follows peer's PARENT_DATA_SET
  - Clock-class change notifications are fired for both instances
  - Lock-state transitions to HOLDOVER then FREERUN
- Restore GM traffic
- Verify both BCs re-lock to GM with clockClass=6
- Repeat tests setting different clockclass(248, 135, 165) on GRANDMASTER_SETTINGS_NP
  between two BC1 and BC2, to mimic different BC winner -- Same behavior

Closes-Bug: 2162870

Signed-off-by: Tara Nath Subedi <Tara.Subedi@windriver.com>
---
 clock.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/clock.c b/clock.c
index b11d42c..4a07af1 100644
--- a/clock.c
+++ b/clock.c
@@ -2437,6 +2437,7 @@ static void handle_state_decision_event(struct clock *c)
 		case PS_GRAND_MASTER:
 			pr_notice("%s: assuming the grand master role",
 				  port_log_name(piter));
+			clock_update_grandmaster(c);
 			event = EV_RS_GRAND_MASTER;
 			break;
 		case PS_MASTER:
